  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042727/assets/e484bfde797b94d298227bac9ae25fb68a3de5698e7f8d8527e37e11cb051a95.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=8?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{8}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=1$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{8}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{8}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8)=1+14=15\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=15.$",
